WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.781
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:16.691
Unit four: Philosophy of Ethics. Today you will discuss abstract ideas using abstract subjects with the passive.

4
00:00:16.991 --> 00:00:19.539
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.872
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:26.089
Moral relativism.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:31.939
Moral relativism means the view that moral judgements depend on culture or context.

8
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.361
Moral relativism struggles to condemn obvious injustice.

9
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.997
Your turn. Say: Moral relativism.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.543
Moral relativism.

11
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:46.737
Utilitarianism.

12
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:53.476
Utilitarianism means the theory that the right action maximises overall well-being.

13
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:57.824
Utilitarianism may justify sacrificing one to save many.

14
00:00:58.417 --> 00:01:01.382
Your turn. Say: Utilitarianism.

15
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:05.745
Utilitarianism.

16
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:07.275
Virtue.

17
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:10.840
Virtue means a stable trait of good character.

18
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.204
Aristotle considered courage a central virtue.

19
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:17.492
Your turn. Say: Virtue.

20
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:21.195
Virtue.

21
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.056
Autonomy.

22
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.512
Autonomy means a person's capacity for self-determination.

23
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:31.440
Medical ethics places great weight on patient autonomy.

24
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.389
Your turn. Say: Autonomy.

25
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:38.292
Autonomy.

26
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.028
Justice.

27
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:44.409
Justice means fairness in the distribution of rights and burdens.

28
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:48.189
Social justice concerns how resources are shared.

29
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:51.494
Your turn. Say: Justice.

30
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:55.381
Justice.

31
00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:56.781
Duty.

32
00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:01.490
Duty means a moral obligation that binds regardless of outcome.

33
00:02:02.083 --> 00:02:04.559
Kant argued that we have a duty never to lie.

34
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.675
Your turn. Say: Duty.

35
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:11.349
Duty.

36
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:13.479
Consequentialism.

37
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:18.422
Consequentialism means judging actions solely by their outcomes.

38
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:22.177
Consequentialism ignores the intentions behind an act.

39
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:26.260
Your turn. Say: Consequentialism.

40
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.519
Consequentialism.

41
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:32.087
Dilemma.

42
00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:37.289
Dilemma means a situation requiring a choice between conflicting values.

43
00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:40.045
Doctors face this dilemma daily.

44
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.488
Your turn. Say: Dilemma.

45
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:47.067
Dilemma.

46
00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:49.038
Deontology.

47
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.464
Deontology means ethics based on rules and duties rather than results.

48
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.211
Deontology holds that some acts are wrong in themselves.

49
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:02.050
Your turn. Say: Deontology.

50
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.831
Deontology.

51
00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:07.654
Pragmatism.

52
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.239
Pragmatism means an approach that judges ideas by practical consequences.

53
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.987
Pragmatism often guides real-world policy.

54
00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:20.282
Your turn. Say: Pragmatism.

55
00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:24.227
Pragmatism.

56
00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:28.696
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:34.208
Visal, in today's seminar, how is justice defined by the main ethical traditions?

58
00:03:35.317 --> 00:03:46.874
Justice is often defined by consequences and duty. Utilitarians focus on outcomes, whereas deontologists focus on obligations.

59
00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:49.530
So which one is right?

60
00:03:50.617 --> 00:04:00.641
Neither is accepted universally. Utilitarianism is criticised for permitting harm to minorities, and deontology is accused of rigidity.

61
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.575
What about virtue ethics?

62
00:04:04.683 --> 00:04:12.023
It is increasingly seen as a middle path, because character is emphasised over rules or calculations.

63
00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.004
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:19.173
Grammar focus: Abstract subject plus passive.

65
00:04:19.473 --> 00:04:20.602
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:21.317 --> 00:04:24.442
Justice is often defined by consequences and duty.

67
00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.741
Utilitarianism is widely criticised for permitting harm to minorities.

68
00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.182
Virtue ethics is increasingly seen as a middle path.

69
00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:46.122
Academic writing often makes an abstract idea the subject and uses the passive to report general views without naming who holds them. Adverbs like 'often', 'widely' and 'increasingly' add precision.

70
00:04:46.883 --> 00:04:51.730
Be careful! Don't say: Utilitarianism is criticised of ignoring rights.

71
00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:57.628
Say: Utilitarianism is criticised for ignoring rights.

72
00:04:57.928 --> 00:05:00.230
We criticise something FOR doing something.

73
00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.709
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.047
What Makes an Action Right?.

75
00:05:06.347 --> 00:05:11.202
Moral philosophy asks whether right and wrong are universal or culturally determined.

76
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.601
Justice is often defined by consequences and duty, reflecting two rival traditions.

77
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.791
Utilitarianism is praised for its practicality but criticised for neglecting individual rights.

78
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:32.211
Deontology, by contrast, insists that certain acts, such as lying, are wrong regardless of their outcomes.

79
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.008
Virtue ethics is increasingly seen as a bridge, because it locates morality in character rather than rules.

80
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.160
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:49.129
Utilitarianism is widely blank for neglecting individual rights.

82
00:05:49.429 --> 00:05:53.953
A, criticise. B, criticised. C, criticising.

83
00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.801
The answer is B: criticised.

84
00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:05.669
'Lying is wrong even if it produces good results.' This view reflects blank.

85
00:06:05.969 --> 00:06:10.458
A, consequentialism. B, deontology. C, pragmatism.

86
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.441
The answer is B: deontology.

87
00:06:17.583 --> 00:06:20.930
A choice between two conflicting moral values is a blank.

88
00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.477
A, dilemma. B, virtue. C, verdict.

89
00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:29.894
The answer is A: dilemma.

90
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.698
Which sentence uses an abstract subject + passive correctly?

91
00:06:34.998 --> 00:06:42.703
A, Autonomy is protected by medical law.. B, Autonomy protects by medical law.. C, Medical law is autonomy protected..

92
00:06:46.267 --> 00:06:49.938
The answer is A: Autonomy is protected by medical law..

93
00:06:51.083 --> 00:06:54.964
According to the reading, virtue ethics locates morality in blank.

94
00:06:55.264 --> 00:06:58.673
A, rules. B, outcomes. C, character.

95
00:07:02.233 --> 00:07:04.221
The answer is C: character.

96
00:07:05.433 --> 00:07:08.492
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.313
Which ethical framework do you find most convincing?

98
00:07:29.117 --> 00:07:30.675
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:30.975 --> 00:08:05.946
Personally, I find virtue ethics the most convincing framework. Unlike utilitarianism, it does not reduce morality to a calculation, and unlike deontology, it allows for judgement in difficult situations. For example, a compassionate doctor will weigh honesty and kindness when delivering bad news. Critics argue that it gives vague guidance, and that is a fair point. However, I believe good character is more reliable than any rigid rule.

100
00:08:06.246 --> 00:08:07.967
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:08.883 --> 00:08:13.765
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract subject plus passive.

102
00:08:14.065 --> 00:08:18.354
Today's value is ethical awareness. Think about what is right before you act.

103
00:08:18.654 --> 00:08:21.005
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

104
00:08:21.305 --> 00:08:24.247
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

